<Project>

  <!--
    Shared .NET analysis baseline for the Stardew integration.

    Every csproj under integrations/stardew/ already sets
    TreatWarningsAsErrors=true, so this file deliberately does NOT set it:
    that priority belongs to each project. What this file fixes is the
    drift in *which analyzer level* each project uses - three of the ten
    csproj set AnalysisLevel themselves and seven did not, which means a
    newly added project silently lands outside analysis coverage.

    These two properties are the official .NET SDK mechanism for keeping
    analyzer configuration consistent across a solution without repeating
    it in every csproj.

    Not set here on purpose:
      - TreatWarningsAsErrors / CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors:
        each project owns its warning policy; enabling it centrally would
        change seven projects at once.
      - Any third-party analyzer package (SonarAnalyzer, StyleCop,
        Meziantou, Roslynator): the SDK's built-in NetAnalyzers are not
        yet used to their full extent, and analyzer NuGet packages add
        restore time and version-conflict surface for no current benefit.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One language version for every project under integrations/stardew/, set here and nowhere
    else. Measured on 2026-11-16 before this line existed: not one of the eleven csproj named
    LangVersion, so all eleven compiled at whatever the SDK defaults for their target
    framework - C# 10 for net6.0 - and nothing in the tree said so. That default is invisible
    and moves with the SDK, which is the property this file replaces: a project added
    tomorrow inherits 12.0 instead of silently compiling at a different language version.

    WHY 12.0 AND NOT 13.0. The ceiling is the pinned compiler, not a preference: global.json
    pins SDK 8.0.424 (rollForward latestPatch -> 8.0.425), whose Roslyn accepts up to C# 12.
    Asking for 13.0 fails the build outright - `error CS1617: invalid option "13.0" for
    /langversion` - so a higher number here is not "more modern", it is a broken build. Raise
    this line only after global.json selects a newer SDK.

    WHY NO POLYSHARP. PolySharp exists to backfill the runtime types modern syntax needs on old
    target frameworks. Measured on net6.0 with TreatWarningsAsErrors (a throwaway net6.0 project
    at LangVersion 12.0): raw string literals, collection expressions over arrays, Span and
    List<T>, `ref readonly` parameters, `u8` literals and primary constructors all compile with
    0 warnings and 0 errors on the bare framework. Only inline arrays need a polyfilled
    `InlineArrayAttribute`, and this tree contains none (`grep InlineArray` over the whole
    Stardew integration: 0 hits). A package that backfills one unused feature is a dependency
    that buys nothing and is re-evaluated the day the first inline array is wanted.

    NOT SET HERE: TargetFramework. net6.0 is fixed by SMAPI - Stardew's mod host loads this
    assembly inside its own .NET 6 process - so it is an external constraint, not a choice this
    file may revisit. `tools/check-dotnet-target-and-language.mjs` asserts both halves.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
